# {{.project_name}}

This Skyulf 0.9.0 Bundle fits and predicts with **{{.engine}}**. Spark reads
bounded Unity Catalog Delta rows and writes predictions. The selected job
compute mode is **{{.compute_mode}}**. `dev`, `test`, `syst` and `prod` use the
same Skyulf workflow code with different target bindings.

## Review your training setup first

Initialization is ordered: **data -> model -> evaluation CV
-> lifecycle -> compute**. Date prompts are hidden when irrelevant. The same
choices can be supplied in a JSON init file.

Setup uses your answers and does not inspect data. Observation-date questions
are hidden for random full-snapshot training; answer-availability dates are
separate and appear only if that filter is enabled. For each used date column,
choose `timestamp` (database instant), `local_timestamp` (clock without timezone),
`date` (day only), or `text` (string). Text asks what it contains and its format.
Timezone is asked only for local clocks/dates; the midnight/reject choice only
for days without a time. A database timestamp needs none of those follow-ups.
CV, cron and cluster details also stay hidden until their option is selected.
These choices guide the questions; runtime validates the actual source values.

Install the matching Skyulf wheel locally, then run from the generated project:

```powershell
python src/preview.py
python src/preview.py --list-models
python src/preview.py --list-preprocessors
python src/preview.py --action train
```

The default preview validates structure, shows the ordered pipeline, and explains
missing manual training pins. `--action train` additionally requires a concrete
source version and any manual dates. `--action train_monthly` checks the automatic
selection policy. Preview executes your trusted Python recipe to list its steps;
it does not itself read data, train or deploy. Keep data access and training out
of module-level code and both recipe functions. The preview
uses the generated dev catalog/schema defaults; for another target pass
`--catalog`, `--input-schema`, `--output-schema`, `--metadata-schema` and
`--resource-suffix` with the same values used by your Bundle target/overrides.
It does not resolve Databricks target variables or check cloud permissions.

Preprocessing is not a setup question. Edit `build_preprocessing()` in
`src/preprocessing.py`: select columns, add Skyulf nodes and reorder steps.
It starts empty. Keep `pipeline.preprocessing` empty in JSON; the Python recipe
supplies the actual Core step list. IDs and metadata defaults come
from the installed Core registry via the list commands above; a registry listing
does not guarantee every option/dependency is usable in your target environment.

For training eligibility, edit `build_pre_split_steps()` in the same Python
file. It starts empty and accepts only Core `DropMissingRows` with an explicit
`subset` (and optional `how`, `threshold`, or `missing_threshold`), or
`ManualBounds` with explicit finite numeric bounds. For example:

```python
def build_pre_split_steps():
    """Keep known targets and plausible ages for training and evaluation."""
    return [
        {"name": "known_target", "transformer": "DropMissingRows",
         "params": {"subset": ["target"], "how": "any"}},
        {"name": "valid_age", "transformer": "ManualBounds",
         "params": {"bounds": {"age": {"lower": 0, "upper": 120}}}},
    ]
```

The named columns must exist in the training source. `age` may be used only
for filtering; it need not be a model input. Bounds require numeric source
columns; Boolean columns are rejected. These filters run after source snapshot/window selection, the optional
seeded source sample, bounded local read, and label-availability selection.
Availability is selected before sampling when sampling is enabled; without a
sample it is selected after the bounded read. Filters run before the final
train/holdout split and fold-local learned preprocessing.
The sample is never refilled: filtering can leave fewer rows than requested.
Both fit and held-out metrics use the remaining eligible population, with
requested steps and per-step exclusions in `pre_split_filters.json`. Missing
training targets require an explicit target filter; otherwise training fails.
Keep learned imputation, scaling and custom code in `build_preprocessing()`.

For example, replace the empty Python recipe with the following steps
after changing `income` and `age` to your actual numeric feature names:

```python
def build_preprocessing():
    """Learn numeric cleanup and scaling from training rows only."""
    return [
        {"name": "impute", "transformer": "SimpleImputer",
         "params": {"columns": ["income", "age"], "strategy": "mean"}},
        {"name": "scale", "transformer": "StandardScaler",
         "params": {"columns": ["income", "age"]}},
    ]
```

For your own transformation, define top-level Calculator/Applier classes in
the same file and return `custom_step(...)` alongside the standard nodes.
The generated `CenterCalculator`/`CenterApplier` example shows both pandas and
Polars handling: fit learns a mean, apply reuses it. Enable it by adding
`example_custom_step("income")` to the returned list. Custom steps must preserve
row count/order and store learned values in the returned fit state. CV fits
these steps separately within each fold.

Training snapshots this file with the fitted pipeline. Local and MLflow loading
restore that code before loading the saved model; scoring, approval and rollback
do not execute the current editable file. Code changes affect the next training
run. This supports one self-contained Python file (up to 64 KiB); imported packages
must be installed on the job. Sibling project modules are not bundled automatically.
Model source and pickle are trusted executable artifacts, not sandboxed code.

The model menu shows only registered models for your selected task. Some models
need optional packages or additional settings (for example, ensemble members);
appearing in the menu is not a guarantee that default settings fit your data.
In `config/workflow.json`, `pipeline.modeling.params` means the `params` object
inside `modeling`: add settings there, or keep `{}` for Core defaults.
For example, with `random_forest_classifier` or `random_forest_regressor`:

```json
"modeling": {
  "type": "random_forest_classifier",
  "params": {"n_estimators": 100, "max_depth": 8, "random_state": 42}
}
```

The native initializer offers a task-specific model menu. Edit preprocessing in
Python and model parameters in JSON. Re-run preview
after editing. Preflight is structural; node-specific data/parameter validity is
still checked by Core during execution. Do not treat preview as a successful fit.

`cv_enabled=true` evaluates fixed parameters on the training partition, refitting
preprocessing within each fold. It never uses the final holdout or selects new
parameters. Choose `cv_type`, `cv_folds`, `cv_shuffle`, `cv_random_state`. Time-series
CV requires a mapped event column and `cv_shuffle=false`; stratified CV requires
classification. Parameter search/Optuna integration is a later task.

`training_window_mode=auto` resolves to full snapshot for random splitting or
rolling calendar for temporal splitting. Choose `fixed_window` or
`rolling_calendar` explicitly to select an observation window **and then** use a
random holdout. Rolling selection has separate `monthly_lookback_months` and
`window_timezone`. Source parsing uses `event_time_format`, `event_time_timezone`,
`event_date_only` (and corresponding `result_*` inputs); these become the two
`*_time_parsing` objects. String formats use numeric Python-style directives.
Leave format/timezone blank for native timezone-aware timestamps; local/date-only
sources need explicit rules. Neither the schedule nor window timezone supplies
an omitted source timezone.

`training_sample_rows` defaults to `null`: no sample, overflow fails. An explicit
size samples eligible source records before splitting; it includes the holdout.
`training_sample_seed` pins repeatable key-based selection. Scoring is never sampled.

## Setup questions in plain language

| Setting | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Training input | Existing rows with features and known answers. |
| Prediction input | Existing rows the model will read to predict; blank reuses training input. This is not the output table. |
| Prediction output | Choose `prediction_table_name` during setup, e.g. `customer_predictions`; blank defaults to a project-named output. The configured output schema and target suffix apply. |
| Record keys | Enter `customer_id` or `customer_id, observation_id`; these identify one row. |
| Feature columns | Enter `income, age`; these are inputs the model learns from. |
| Training version | Delta snapshot number from the source table's History, such as 12. It pins data, not a model version. `null` means configure it before manual training; scheduled runs select latest. |
| Source window | All rows (`full_snapshot`), fixed observation dates (`fixed_window`), or recent complete months (`rolling_calendar`). `auto` picks all rows for random splitting and rolling months for temporal splitting. |
| Result availability | A column such as `claim_confirmed_at` tells when each answer became known. Availability filtering excludes answers known after `result_cutoff`; scheduled runs use their invocation time. |
| Text date format | `10/08/2026 14:30` uses `%d/%m/%Y %H:%M`; `2026-08-10` uses `%Y-%m-%d`. Database timestamp/date columns need no text format. |
| Source timezone | For a clock value with no offset, enter its location, e.g. `Europe/Copenhagen`. A value ending in `+02:00` already identifies its offset. |
| Date only | `reject` stops; `midnight` treats a date with no clock time as 00:00 in the declared source timezone. |
| Training sample | 10,000 means at most 10,000 eligible rows total, then split into training/test. `null` disables sampling; read limits still apply. |
| CV | Several evaluations on different training-row splits; final test data stays separate. It evaluates settings, not parameter tuning. |

All date fields can remain unused for a random full-snapshot split with result
availability filtering disabled. Date parsing, data-window timezone and job
clock timezone are separate because they answer different questions.

## What deployment creates

`src/workflow.py` and `src/score.py` are thin, fixed-role entry points. The
installed `job_runtime` adapter handles widgets/JSON and calls Core's existing
`local_workflow` and `prediction_output` services. Customize preprocessing in
`src/preprocessing.py` and model/workflow settings in `config/workflow.json`;
common workflow changes belong in the Skyulf wheel.

`bundle deploy` uploads the wheel, notebook and configuration, and creates
two jobs. It creates **no** Unity Catalog table or registered model.
The initialization choice `retraining_mode=scheduled` adds an enabled
schedule to the existing `train` job; the default `manual` choice adds none.
`pause_status: UNPAUSED` keeps the selected schedule active even in dev mode.
It starts at the next matching cron time after deployment; initialization only
creates local files.
Your Quartz cron controls frequency. For example, `0 0 3 1 1,7 ?` runs at 03:00
on January 1 and July 1 in the chosen schedule timezone. There is no additional
monthly timer overriding it. The runtime action is still named `train_monthly`;
it selects the latest snapshot and derives configured data windows at invocation.
The data window is independent: running every six months does not automatically
read six months of data. Independent score scheduling remains later work.

| Job | Run it when | UC effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `train` | Train, approve, reject or roll back using `lifecycle_action` | Training registers a candidate; operator actions reuse existing versions |
| `score` | A model is pinned and rows need scoring | Appends rows or builds a new generation |

In both selection modes, each registered contender becomes `@challenger`
before comparison. Model-version tags show `validation_status` (`pending`,
`passed`, `rejected`, or `error`), `validation_reason`, and `promotion_status`.
A tied or worse candidate remains challenger; a newer contender replaces the
pointer while earlier version evidence remains. Only the serialized train job
identity may write these aliases. Restrict other writers before running train;
table-free alias admission relies on that externally enforced ownership.

Run and model-version tags use `train_data_version`, `test_data_version`,
`train_data_destination`, `test_data_destination`, `model_type`, and
`candidate_date_tag`. Both data versions refer to the pinned source snapshot;
`split_strategy` names the evaluation split. Temporal runs also record
`train_start`, `test_start`, and `data_end`; date-free runs omit these tags.
Optional `risk_category` is selected at initialization and stays editable in
config/workflow.json. Leaving it blank omits the tag; it never changes promotion gates.
For policy compute, select `PayingRegNo` as the cost tag key when required by
your company and provide the approved number as the cost tag value.
The complete dataset identity is in the run's training_data.json artifact.
Technical promotion event tags contain recovery receipts; do not edit them.

## Model selection and operator actions

This project uses `score_model_selection={{.score_model_selection}}`,
`promotion_policy={{.promotion_policy}}`, and `score_handoff={{.score_handoff}}`.
These choices remain editable in `config/workflow.json`. Changing `score_handoff`
also requires updating `deployed_score_handoff` in both notebook tasks in
`resources/workflow.jobs.yml` and redeploying. A mismatch fails before execution.
Keep `workflow_contract: "1"` fixed in both tasks; it is not a run parameter.

| Setting | Choices |
| --- | --- |
| `score_model_selection` | `pinned_version` keeps `model_version`; `champion` resolves the controlled alias once per score run |
| `promotion_policy` | `manual_approval` waits for an operator; `automatic` promotes only after quality gates pass |
| `score_handoff` | `disabled`, or `after_alias_change` to call the existing score job after champion initialization, promotion or rollback |

All four selector/promotion combinations are supported. Promotion never rewrites
a scoring pin. A first champion requires a numeric absolute `quality_threshold`,
including manual approval; subsequent candidates also need positive improvement
meeting `min_improvement`. Configure the metric and gates for your task before fit.

Use the **train job's Run now parameters** for every lifecycle action. It is
queued with one active run, so operator actions share training's writer.
Configuration and lifecycle policy overrides are not accepted as run parameters.

| `lifecycle_action` | Additional parameters |
| --- | --- |
| `train` / `train_monthly` | Leave operator evidence fields empty |
| `approve` | `candidate_version`, `expected_champion_version` |
| `reject` | Same evidence plus `rejection_reason` |
| `rollback` | `promotion_receipt_json`, `expected_champion_version` |

Training output contains `next_actions.approve` and `next_actions.reject` in
manual mode. Copy the relevant fields, adding a reason for rejection. Explicit
`none` is used for a first champion; blank is not accepted. A completed promotion
returns `next_actions.rollback`, including a ready-to-copy receipt JSON string.
Copy that string's value into `promotion_receipt_json`; do not edit receipt fields.
Approval/rejection resolves the full comparison digest from the named model
version's committed receipt. You do not copy a hash. Missing/stale evidence and
changed comparison artifacts still fail the strict Core checks. Older callers
can continue supplying `comparison_sha256` explicitly.
The executed notebook shows a readable summary and next-action parameter tables;
expand Technical details for JSON. The standard Notebook exited JSON is retained
for API consumers. A no-op manifest describes the previous prediction write.
Initial champion creation has no prior champion and cannot be rolled back.
Clear fields unrelated to the selected action. All prior evidence is verified
against the registry; changing an alias directly in Catalog UI is not approval.

Operator actions never retrain/register/upload another model. A retry of the
same still-current committed operation returns its receipt. Rejected or tied
candidates do not trigger score. Manual training also waits for approval.
With handoff enabled, a completed champion change triggers score using the
configured selector, including an unchanged pinned version if selected.
The score notebook has a fixed score role. It ignores inherited lifecycle
parameters from the parent job and rejects role/action override attempts.
For one independent score run, set its `score_model_version` parameter to a
positive concrete version number. This overrides the selector for that run
without redeployment; leave it blank to use the configured selector. It does
not change the saved pin or champion. The lifecycle handoff explicitly clears
this parameter so it always uses the configured selection policy.

Handoff and Delta publication remain separate operations. If score fails,
retry the independent score job or repair only the handoff task; do not rerun
training. An unknown alias write leaves a pending marker requiring reconciliation.
`previous_challenger` retains displaced candidates and clears if that version
becomes champion/challenger again. Model versions and evidence remain available.

In scheduled mode, the schedule starts enabled. Its Quartz cron and IANA timezone
are selected at initialization and remain editable as
`retraining_cron_expression` and `retraining_timezone_id` Bundle variables.
The default is 03:00 UTC on the third day of each month; choose any supported
Quartz cron cadence. Each run captures the latest concrete Delta source
version. `training_window_mode` independently selects `full_snapshot`,
`fixed_window` or `rolling_calendar`. Rolling selection uses the explicit
`window_timezone` and `monthly_lookback_months` (initial temporal project:
UTC and four months). With a temporal split the last completed month is the
holdout, included in that count. Random splitting divides the selected data by
`test_size`; a fixed window never advances with the schedule. If result
availability filtering is enabled, its separate cutoff is the job invocation
instant. The captured Delta version is pinned at run start; it is not a
historical snapshot as of the observation cutoff. If the source has too few
labeled rows in either partition, training fails without publishing a model.
The run compares against the concrete version behind `@champion` if present;
a missing alias means first-model training. An explicit `champion_version`
must match the current champion or training fails before fit. Registry access failures still
fail the job. Retry runs can create additional candidate versions.
Manual approval mode retains the candidate until a separate operator action.

`training_table` is the existing labeled input. `score_source_table` is the
existing input to predict. Initialization accepts `source_table_name` and an
optional `score_source_table_name` within the target's input schema. A blank
source name uses `<project_name>_source`; a blank scoring source reuses training.
You may give them different UC names when the labeled training data and new
inference data have different lifecycles. Neither is created by this Bundle.
`prediction_table` is the physical output table in `incremental_append` mode.
In `full_rebuild` mode it is a stable **view**, backed by physical Delta
generations named `<prediction_table>_v<model_version>`. A successful `score`
fills a new generation before switching the view; previous generations stay
available. The first full score creates one physical table and the view.
Each generation records the registered model name, version and artifact digest.
An existing generation with different provenance is rejected. If you change
the registered model name while reusing the same numeric version, choose a
different logical prediction name or a new model version before scoring.
Moving an already deployed append-mode table to full mode under the same
logical name is rejected; choose a new view name or migrate deliberately.
`model_name` is a registered UC model, not a table. The Bundle creates no
admission table.
The `record_key_columns` initialization input names existing source keys as
comma-separated text (for example, `customer_id, observation_id`).
The generated config stores it as `["customer_id", "observation_id"]`.
`score` copies them into the prediction table for SQL lookups. Each key must be
non-null and `STRING` or `BIGINT`; the combination must uniquely identify records
across all scored rows, including later inserts. The resulting
`record_key_columns` remains editable in `config/workflow.json`.
With no key input, `entity_id` remains the example source-column name.

`result_available_at_column` names the source timestamp recording when each
row's target became available. It defaults to null and is required only with
`filter_unavailable_results: true`, together with an aware `result_cutoff`.
`event_column` names the observation timestamp for `split_strategy: temporal`;
`start`, `holdout_start` and `cutoff` apply to it. Rows can have different result
dates. Result availability is independent of the observation window.
The library and saved training settings use these same names directly.
Regenerate projects and retrain models created before this pre-production
rename; earlier column settings and saved training evidence are not converted.
The default `split_strategy: random` requires no date columns. Set `test_size`
(default 0.2), `random_state` (42) and optionally classification `stratify`
(false). Stable record-key sorting precedes Core splitting. Approval replays the
saved snapshot, split policy and holdout-key digest; input row ordering cannot
silently change evaluation membership. A different snapshot can change the split.

Optional Basic-model CV is configured in this same workflow file with
`cv_enabled`, `cv_folds` (2â€“20), `cv_type`, `cv_shuffle`, and `cv_random_state`.
It defaults to disabled. `stratified_k_fold` is for classification; `k_fold` and
`shuffle_split` also support regression. `time_series_split` uses the normalized
`event_column` from window selection and requires shuffle disabled. The timestamp
is removed before fold feature engineering; tied timestamps crossing a fold
boundary are rejected. Core refits preprocessing inside each fold, independently
of the saved final model. MLflow contains `cross_validation.json` and `cv_*`
metrics; the `heldout_*` promotion metrics still use untouched final test rows.
This is evaluation with fixed parameters, not Optuna/search support.

| Split | Availability filter | Date settings |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Random | Disabled | None; all targets must be known. |
| Random | Enabled | Result column and `result_cutoff`. |
| Temporal | Disabled | Event column and ordered `start`, `holdout_start`, `cutoff`. |
| Temporal | Enabled | Both sets; independent observation and result cutoffs. |

Null or late result times are excluded when filtering is enabled. Eligible
null targets fail. With filtering disabled, every target must be nonnull.
Keep inactive date fields null and parsing rules at defaults; contradictory
settings fail rather than silently switching policies. Configure
`event_time_parsing` and `result_time_parsing` independently in
`config/workflow.json`. Defaults accept native Spark `TIMESTAMP` instants.
For strings use an explicit numeric Python format, for example
`{"format": "%d/%m/%Y %H:%M:%S", "timezone": "Europe/Copenhagen", "date_only": "reject"}`.
For offset-bearing strings use `%z` and leave timezone null. Native local-clock
`TIMESTAMP_NTZ` needs a timezone but no format. Date-only inputs additionally
need `date_only: "midnight"`; they represent local midnight, not UTC by default.
Full year/month/day are required; unknown formats and DST gaps/overlaps fail.
These are Python format directives, not Spark `yyyy-MM-dd` patterns.

Source dates are validated before filtering, so invalid events cannot disappear
outside a training window. Null result availability remains unavailable. The
saved training spec retains the parsing rules for approval replay. Row/byte
budgets bound driver materialization; source validation can scan the snapshot
in Spark. The source timezone is independent of the job's cron timezone.

Initialization never creates an ID in the source. Keep key
columns out of `input_columns`; they identify predictions, not model features.
The score job has `max_concurrent_runs: 1`; grant write access to its physical
prediction targets only to this job's identity. In full mode the job also
needs permission to create and own the active view. Other jobs must not write
the targets.
Skyulf Core's shared-admission option remains available when multiple jobs
need to publish to one target.

## Prepare the project

1. Build a matching, trusted wheel from a Skyulf checkout with `uv build
   --wheel --no-build-isolation --out-dir <this-project>/dist skyulf-core`.
2. Edit `config/workflow.json` for real source/model names, row keys, model
   inputs, estimator, split policy and size limits. The
   initialized task is **{{.task}}**: regression starts with linear regression
   and RMSE; classification defaults to logistic regression and accuracy. The
   initializer lets you select other task-compatible models/metrics. Enter
   `input_columns` as comma-separated names, then edit
   `src/preprocessing.py` for preprocessing and `pipeline.modeling` for the estimator. Feature
   engineering is not inferred automatically. Set a task-compatible metric
   and quality threshold, and positive `max_rows`/`max_input_mb` transfer limits.
   `max_input_mb` defaults to 64 MiB (one unit = 1024 * 1024 bytes), limiting
   local input size rather than total training RAM. `max_rows` bounds input
   before train/test splitting; overflow fails without implicit truncation.
   Optional `training_sample_rows`/`training_sample_seed` select eligible records
   on Spark before local transfer. The sample count includes final holdout rows;
   scoring is never sampled. Leave `training_sample_rows` null to use all data.
   In an initialization config file, supply those limits as integer strings
   (for example, `"max_rows": "10000"`); generated workflow limits are numbers.
   For manual training, explicitly set `training_version`. Random splitting
   needs no dates. Temporal splitting requires an event column and aware
   `start < holdout_start < cutoff`. Availability filtering requires a result
   column and independent `result_cutoff`. Monthly training derives its snapshot
   per run and derives observation boundaries only for `rolling_calendar`,
   using `window_timezone` independently of the cron timezone.
   Enable Delta Change
   Data Feed on the scoring source before new rows arrive. Check that the
   source's initial rows are the rows you intend to score: the first `score`
   run predicts **all** existing source rows, including labeled training rows
   when one table is used for both purposes.
3. Authenticate a Databricks CLI profile and validate/deploy the `dev` target:

   ```powershell
   databricks bundle validate --strict -t dev --profile <profile>
   databricks bundle deploy -t dev --profile <profile>
   ```

   `dev` uses the profile's workspace host and the catalog/schema chosen at
   initialization. The three other targets contain **different placeholder
   hosts and catalogs**. Replace each with an approved value and use its own
   profile before validation or deployment. Credentials are never saved here.
   The generated target variables also let each environment use different
   input, output and metadata schemas.

{{if eq .compute_mode "policy_cluster"}}
The selected policy-backed jobs look up the policy name supplied during
initialization. Confirm its runtime, node type and cost tag in each workspace.
An example init config lives in `skyulf-core/templates/databricks/examples/policy-init.example.json`
in the Skyulf checkout. Its placeholders must be reviewed before use.
{{else}}
The generated jobs use Databricks serverless Python environment 4. Policy
clusters are a separate compute choice at project initialization.
{{end}}

## Existing-project migration

New projects use `config_version: 1` and an explicit `task`. Before upgrading
an existing project, review its source snapshot, date split, task, metric and
limits. Replace legacy `model_selection_mode` with explicit
`score_model_selection`, `promotion_policy`, and `score_handoff`; do not retain
both formats. The helper
`skyulf.integrations.databricks.workflow_config.migrate_workflow_config(config,
task="regression", score_handoff="disabled")` returns a migrated copy; select
the task and handoff deliberately, then review and save the result.
Add the fixed notebook contract fields and score run parameter
from this template, then validate and redeploy. Keep row keys and prediction
columns compatible with existing outputs; incompatible changes require a new
prediction target or an explicit data migration.

## First run and later increments

1. Run `train` against a pinned labeled Delta version. It logs held-out MLflow
   metrics and registers a concrete model version.
2. In manual mode, approve the existing candidate if champion-based scoring
   is desired. Pinned scoring instead uses the explicit `model_version`;
   update and redeploy that config to change its saved pin, or use
   `score_model_version` for one run. Run `score` independently,
   or inspect the optional handoff following a successful champion transition.
   Scoring verifies the source, Change Data Feed, model
   outputs, row keys and any existing target schema before writing. It creates
   only a missing physical prediction table; it never overwrites an existing
   table or creates the source. Full mode then creates or updates its managed
   view after a complete nonempty score. The initial row check is bounded by `max_rows`, and
   scoring enforces `max_rows` and `max_input_mb` for each local transfer.
3. Add new rows to the existing source Delta table and run `score`
   again. An unchanged replay is a no-op. No date or source-version argument
   is entered for each run. Source updates or deletes require a separate
   rescore policy.

{{if eq .score_model_selection "pinned_version"}}
`score` uses its configured concrete model version unless the run supplies
`score_model_version`. Training a new
candidate or changing a registry alias alone does not change predictions.
{{else}}
`score` loads the concrete champion version at run start unless the run supplies
`score_model_version`. A later alias change
cannot alter that run's pinned model. Score remains independently runnable
for new source rows, regardless of the configured handoff choice.
{{end}}
When a score run selects v2, `incremental_append` leaves v1 rows intact
and scores only new source inserts with v2. `full_rebuild` scores every current
source row into a new v2 table, switches the view only after success, then
uses that table's Delta receipt to append future inserts. A failed rebuild
leaves the old view active. The new generation must retain the active output
schema; an incompatible model needs a new logical view or an explicit migration.
The training schedule uses the same promotion and handoff choices. There is no online
endpoint or Spark-native FE/model execution in this starting project.

### Explicit retry policy

Generated notebook tasks set `max_retries: 0`; serverless tasks additionally set
`disable_auto_optimization: true` because serverless auto-optimization can add
retries independently. A training retry can register another candidate version.
Inspect failed run evidence and registry state before rerunning; retry score
separately after an already-committed approval. This is not an exactly-once
training guarantee. See the [Databricks serverless retry behavior](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-serverless-jobs).
